/** @page dev Development

@section dev-objective Objective

The objective is to create a videography software suite, that allows to record 
scientifically relevant data from different types of video sources including 
supplemental information and data streams (such as timing, audio, etc.). 
Ultimately we aim at a modular framework that allows to supplement real-time 
features, such as object recognition combined with feedback.




@section dev-design Design

To facilitate remote data acquisition, which may happen on a headless system,
recording and displaying data are separated into a backend (the recording 
service or daemon, respectively) and the frontend (the user interface). The 
following diagram gives an idea of the components and the acquisition data flow: 

@image html overview.svg


@subsection dev-backend Backend

The backend must—with high reliability and speed—record video frames and 
additional data from various sources. It should be implemented in the sense
of a system service (which also means, it should automatically start on system
boot). The service must not crash on partial failures (such as device errors) 
but continue to operate.

@subsubsection dev-backend-monitoring Monitoring

<b>Requires</b>:<br/>
@ref core-logging<br/>
@ref core-sysinfo<br/>

@subsubsection dev-backend-devices Devices

<b>Requires</b>:<br/>
@ref core-plugins<br/>

@subsubsection dev-backend-storage Storage

<b>Requires</b>:<br/>
@ref core-storage<br/>

@subsubsection dev-backend-realtime Real-time interface


@subsubsection dev-backend-transport Transport


@subsubsection dev-backend-settings Settings


@subsection dev-frontend Frontend






@section dev-impl Implementation


@subsection dev-lang Language(s) and configuration

Core functionality should be provided in C++ (at least [C++20](https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/20)). 
Note, however, that the software must be compilable on at least Linux® and 
Windows®. For user-interface applications [Qt6](https://www.qt.io/product/framework) 
forms the basis. 

While the backend (the recording service or daemon, respectively) will rely
on compiled code, interfaces to other languages (mainly Python, but also
Matlab and more) will be provided. 
The frontend (user interface), though, will be a mixture of Python and C++ 
(see [Qt for Python](https://doc.qt.io/qtforpython-6/)). With respect to
the backend, it should be discussed if a terminal UI should be provided
to simplify configuration or supervision, especially on a headless, remote
machine.

For project configuration we use [CMake](https://cmake.org/cmake/help/latest/).


@subsection dev-git Version control

The "main" branch remains in the hands of BBO authors. External developers may 
not without explicit permission commit there. Instead, they must either use an 
arranged (read "agreed upon") branch or use github's pull-request feature.

@TBD{make user-interface independent (see vidre-gui on github)?}


@subsection dev-docs Documentation

API documentation is generated using [Doxygen](https://doxygen.nl/manual/index.html)
and published regularly on https://bbo-lab.github.io/vidre.

Documentation is, however, not limited to the source code. @TBD

Document as much as necessary, but not more (in case of doubt, follow
[XKCD 1343](https://xkcd.com/1343/) but not [XKCD 1481](https://xkcd.com/1481/)).



